{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffsetBarOddClass","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffsetBarOddClass","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":21,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.arithMean_bondConst_eq_midpoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.arithMean_bondConst_eq_midpoint","kind":"theorem","line":360,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L360-L369","doc":"**The arithmetic mean's bond constant is the midpoint of the other two.**\n\nThe p-p bond has `d = 0` and the n-n bond `d = -h`; the `ε = 0` p-n bond has\n`d = -h/2`, their midpoint.  Since the trace is linear this is the whole\ncontent of \"arithmetic mean\" at the element level: the bond is\n`σ - (h/2)·1 = (σ + σ⁻¹)/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.quarter_sub_fPP_mul_fNN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.quarter_sub_fPP_mul_fNN","kind":"theorem","line":350,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L350-L358","doc":"The same identity in the form that gives the bound directly:\n`1/4 - f_pp·f_nn` is a square, so `f_pp·f_nn ≤ 1/4` over any ordered field,\nwith equality exactly at `q² = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.one_sub_fPP_mul_fNN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.one_sub_fPP_mul_fNN","kind":"theorem","line":300,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L300-L348","doc":"**The product of the FACTORS is not the trace of the product of the BONDS,\nand misses it by at least `3/4` at every `q`.**\n\nThis is the statement that decides the arithmetic-vs-geometric question, so it\nis worth being explicit about what the two sides are.\n\n* The two like-flavour bonds are the Hecke elements `σ` (p-p, `d = 0`) and\n  `σ - h·1 = σ⁻¹` (n-n, `d = -h`) — see `bondD` and\n  `QOU.Hecke.ClosedForm.interfaceFactor_eq_bond_expectation`.  They are\n  **mutually inverse**, so their product in the algebra is the identity, whose\n  Markov-trace expectation is `τ(1) = 1` (the normalisation that\n  `f_k = z + d_k` already assumes).\n* Their two *factors* are `f_pp` and `f_nn`, and this theorem says\n  `1 - f_pp·f_nn` is a square plus `3/4`.  So over any ordered field\n  `f_pp·f_nn ≤ 1/4`, never `1`, and the two sides differ by a factor of at\n  least four.\n\nHence the Markov trace is **not** multiplicative on these bonds — it could not\nbe, since a bond and its inverse would have to have reciprocal traces — and the\nclosed form's product over bonds is a product of *traces*, not the trace of a\n*product*.\n\n**The consequence for the fork.**  The case for the geometric mean was that the\nclosed form is a product, so the multiplicative midpoint of two neighbouring\nfactors is the natural interpolation.  That argument takes a geometric mean at\nthe level of *factors* while borrowing its motivation from a multiplicative\nstructure that exists only at the level of *elements* — and the two levels are\nnot connected here, by this theorem.  At the element level the multiplicative\nmidpoint of `σ` and `σ⁻¹` is the identity, with trace `1`, which is not the\ngeometric mean `z/q²` of the factors (`0.497` at the proton pin) nor anything\nelse in the family.\n\nThe arithmetic mean has no such gap: the Markov trace is **linear**, so\n`(f_pp + f_nn)/2` is exactly the trace of `(σ + σ⁻¹)/2`, the skein symmetriser\nthat `QOU.BraidKnot.interface_knot_markov_z_bar_symmetric` is already stated\nabout, and `arithMean_bondConst_eq_midpoint` records that its bond constant is\nthe midpoint of the other two.\n\n**Scope.**  This is a statement about which of the two candidate interpolations\nhas algebraic backing, not a derivation of the physical offset.  It removes the\nstructural argument for the geometric mean; it does **not** establish that\n`ε = 0` is correct, and the colour-tube correction `δ/4` sits on top of the\narithmetic mean either way. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.fPP_mul_fNN_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.fPP_mul_fNN_eq","kind":"theorem","line":290,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L290-L298","doc":"**The product of the two like-flavour factors, in closed form.**\n\n`f_pp · f_nn = (q/(q²+1))²`, which is `(z/q²)²` — consistent with `geomMean_sq`,\nand the reason the geometric mean is `z/q²` in the first place. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_pole_residue_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_pole_residue_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":282,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L282-L286","doc":"The residue at the classical point is `-3/2`, hence nonzero: the pole is\ngenuine and of order exactly two. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_pole_residue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_pole_residue","kind":"theorem","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L267-L280","doc":"**…and it has a double pole at `q = 1`, so the class does not contain it.**\n\nClearing the candidate pole leaves `-6q³/(q²+1)²`, a quantity regular at\n`q = 1`.  Together with `geomMean_pole_residue_at_one` (its value there is\n`-3/2`, not `0`) this says the pole is genuine and of order exactly two: it does\nnot cancel.  With `weightForm_cubic_factor` — every regular `E` gives an offset\ndivisible by `(q-1)³` — that is the sharp statement that the geometric-mean\ncandidate is unreachable inside `ε = q·δ·E/4`, and hence that the frame is\nnarrower than the set of bar-even p-n factors it was meant to parametrise. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_via_weightForm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_via_weightForm","kind":"theorem","line":258,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L258-L265","doc":"**The `E` that would reach the geometric mean.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_offset_barOdd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_offset_barOdd","kind":"theorem","line":248,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L248-L256","doc":"That offset is bar-odd, as the criterion requires: substituting `q ↦ q⁻¹`\nreturns its negative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_offset","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_offset","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L240-L246","doc":"**The offset realising the geometric mean** is\n`ε = -(q²-1)/(2(q²+1))`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.geomMean_sq","kind":"theorem","line":227,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L227-L238","doc":"**The geometric mean of the bar orbit is `z/q²`**: `(z/q²)² = z·(z - h)`.\n\n`f_pp = z` and `f_nn = z - h = z/q⁴` are a single bar orbit\n(`ClosedForm.markovZ_bar_eq_shift`), so their product is the bar-norm `z²/q⁴`\nand its square root `z/q²` is bar-even.  It is therefore an admissible p-n\nfactor by the criterion — a second structurally-derived candidate alongside the\narithmetic mean `z - h/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.likeFlavour_bond_not_barInvariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.likeFlavour_bond_not_barInvariant","kind":"theorem","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L209-L223","doc":"**The like-flavour bonds are a bar orbit, not two fixed points.**\n\n`σ` is the offset-`1/2` member of the family and `σ⁻¹` the offset-`(-1/2)` one;\ntheir bond constants are `0` and `-h`, and the bar defect of each is `h` in\nmagnitude but neither satisfies the invariance criterion — instead the bar\ncarries each onto the other.  Stated as the failure of\n`bond_barInvariant_iff_barOdd` at `ε' = ε = 1/2`: the defect is `0`, not `h`.\n\nThis is the algebra-level source of `QOU.Hecke.ClosedForm.fPP_bar_eq_fNN` /\n`fNN_bar_eq_fPP`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.bond_barInvariant_iff_barOdd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.bond_barInvariant_iff_barOdd","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L189-L207","doc":"**The p-n bond is KL-bar-invariant exactly when the offset is bar-odd.**\n\nSo the bar-evenness of the interface *factor* is not a property of the trace: it\nis the shadow of an invariance of the bond *element*, and the two statements\ncarry the same content.  Requires `h ≠ 0`, i.e. `q² ≠ 1` — at the classical\npoint every bond is invariant for trivial reasons. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.bondConst_bar_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.bondConst_bar_sub","kind":"theorem","line":177,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L177-L187","doc":"**Bar defect of the bond.**\n\nThe Kazhdan–Lusztig bar sends `σ ↦ σ⁻¹ = σ - h` and `q ↦ q⁻¹`, so\n`bar(σ + d·1) = σ + (d' - h)·1` where `d'` is the bar image of the constant.\nIts difference from the original constant is `(1 - ε - ε')·h`, using `bar h = -h`\n(`ClosedForm.heckeH_bar`).  Invariance is `d' - h = d`, i.e. this difference\nequalling `h`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.fPN_eq_markovZ_add_bondConst","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.fPN_eq_markovZ_add_bondConst","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L169-L175","doc":"`f_pn` is the trace `z + d` of the bond, as\n`QOU.Hecke.ClosedForm.interfaceFactor_eq_bond_expectation` has it for the\nlike-flavour bonds. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.bondConst","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.bondConst","kind":"def","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L165-L167","doc":"Constant term of the p-n bond element `σ + d·1` whose Markov trace is\n`f_pn`: from `f_pn = z + d` and `f_pn = z - (1/2 - ε)·h`, `d = -(1/2 - ε)·h`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.weightForm_cubic_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.weightForm_cubic_factor","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L147-L161","doc":"**Every member of the class with `E` regular at `q = 1` vanishes to third\norder there**, because `δ` does.\n\n`δ` carries the factor `(q-1)²` explicitly and a further `(q-1)` inside\n`1 - q⁻⁴ = (q⁴-1)/q⁴`; pulling all three out leaves a cofactor in which `q = 1`\nmay be substituted freely.  Consequently a regular-`E` repair moves `f_pn` from\nthe strict-averaged `z - h/2` by `O((q-1)³)` — the same order as the canonical\n`δ/4` it is meant to replace.  This is what excludes the geometric-mean\ncandidate of §3 from the class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.weightForm_barOdd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.weightForm_barOdd","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L133-L145","doc":"**Absorbing the weight makes the offset bar-odd, for every bar-even `E`.**\n\n`bar q = q⁻¹`, `bar δ = -q²·δ` (`PnInterfaceBarStructure`, `ClosedForm.flavorDelta_bar`)\nand `bar E = E` by hypothesis, so the bar image of `ε = q·δ·E/4` is\n`q⁻¹·(-q²·δ)·E/4 = -ε`.\n\nThe bar images are supplied as the substituted values; the statement is the\nresulting identity.  Note there is **no** hypothesis on `E` beyond bar-evenness\nhaving already been used to write `E` on both sides: the criterion of\n`pnWeighted_barEven_iff` selects nothing within this class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.fPN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.fPN","kind":"def","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L127-L129","doc":"The weighted p-n interface factor with offset `ε`:\n`f_pn = (1/2 + ε)·z + (1/2 - ε)·(z - h) = z - (1/2 - ε)·h`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.flavorDelta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.flavorDelta","kind":"def","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L124-L125","doc":"The proven leading flavour split, `δ = (q-1)²·(1 - q⁻⁴)/3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.heckeH","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.heckeH","kind":"def","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L121-L122","doc":"The Hecke skein parameter, `h = q - q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.markovZ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.PnOffset.markovZ","kind":"def","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/PnOffsetBarOddClass.lean#L118-L119","doc":"The Markov parameter in convention D, `z = q³/(q²+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}